webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.ashfield.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-05","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.ashfield.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-23","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.ashfield.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-30","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.ashfield.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-06-25","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.ashfield.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-27","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.ashfield.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-27","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.ashfield.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.ashfield.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.ashfield.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Ashfield's finances dependent on property income as debt service costs rise","lead":"The council's treasury management papers show that the cost of financing capital spending (money spent on buildings, roads and equipment) is forecast to rise from 11.31 per cent of day-to-day spending in 2026-27 to 15.07 per cent by 2028-29. However, the council's portfolio of investment properties is expected to generate sufficient income to substantially reduce the effective burden. The figures would fall to 9.45 per cent in 2026-27 and 6.63 per cent by 2028-29 when property income is included. The sustainability of this financial position depends on the properties maintaining current valuations and rental yields, and on the council's capital spending proceeding as planned.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Treasury Management 2026/27 - Q1 April - June 2026","paper_url":"https://ashfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s38589/38589.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Enc. 1 for Treasury Management 2026/27 - Q1 April - June 2026","paper_url":"https://ashfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s38590/38590.pdf","headline":"Minimum Revenue Provision policy for pre-2008 borrowing revised to 50-year annuity method, replacing 4% reducing balance approach which extended over 300 years","detail":"Council has adopted more prudent MRP methodology charging MRP on pre-2008 supported borrowing over 50-year annuity basis aligned to equivalent PWLB rate, rather than previous approach which would have extended amortisation beyond 300 years. Post-2008 borrowing uses annuity method for assets over 10 years life, straight-line for shorter-life assets.","category":"governance","matters":"More prudent MRP approach accelerates retirement of historic debt and improves long-term financial sustainability through faster de-leveraging","figures":"50-year PWLB rate used for annuity calculation; straight-line method for assets under 10 years","quote":"For pre 2008 supported borrowing, the Council has moved to a 50-year Annuity method, charging MRP based on a corresponding 50-year PWLB borrowing rate. This is more prudent than the previous 4% reducing balance as this calculation extends to over 300 years.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Enc. 1 for Treasury Management 2026/27 - Q1 April - June 2026","paper_url":"https://ashfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s38590/38590.pdf","headline":"PWLB 10-year rates fell from 5.62% (1 April) to 5.55% (30 June 2026); Bank Rate forecast to remain at 3.75% this year then fall to 3.25% by spring 2028","detail":"Gilt yields showed volatility in Q1, peaking at 5.18% in mid-May before falling to 4.76% by quarter end. MUFG forecasts Bank Rate at 3.75% through 2026, falling to 3.5% by end 2027, and 3.25% by spring 2028. Forecast assumes weak labour market and subdued growth will prevent second-round inflation effects.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Lower interest rate forecasts reduce expected cost of future borrowing; current quarter rates better than earlier in year, improving refinancing conditions","figures":"PWLB 10-year rate range Q1 2026: 5.50-5.98%; 1-year range 4.75-5.17%; 10-year average 5.71%","quote":"The 10-year Gilt yield has generally traced that move, falling back to 4.7% in June. This rally in Gilts has also been supported by the Bank of England keeping rates on hold","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Enc. 1 for Treasury Management 2026/27 - Q1 April - June 2026","paper_url":"https://ashfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s38590/38590.pdf","headline":"Capital programme of \u00a368.7m in 2026/27 requires \u00a315.2m new borrowing; Capital Financing Requirement growing through 2029/30","detail":"Capital expenditure totals \u00a368.7m in 2026/27 (\u00a350.2m General Fund, \u00a318.5m HRA). Unfinanced capital expenditure of \u00a315.2m requires new borrowing. Capital Financing Requirement is forecast to grow from \u00a3175.4m (March 2026) to \u00a3176.5m (March 2030) before declining as Minimum Revenue Provision charges exceed new borrowing.","category":"capital","matters":"Demonstrates substantial sustained borrowing requirement extending over multiple years, progressively increasing council indebtedness","figures":"\u00a368.701m total capital expenditure; \u00a315.208m borrowing requirement (2026/27); CFR growth from \u00a3175.4m to \u00a3176.5m over four-year period","quote":"Table 3 below summarises how the capital expenditure plans will be financed by capital or revenue resources. Any shortfall of resources results in a borrowing need.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Enc. 1 for Treasury Management 2026/27 - Q1 April - June 2026","paper_url":"https://ashfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s38590/38590.pdf","headline":"Investment property income offsets financing costs by 1.9-2.4 percentage points, materially supporting financial sustainability","detail":"Commercial property income reduces the financing cost burden from 11.31% to 9.45% in 2026/27, from 14.61% to 7.71% in 2027/28, and from 15.07% to 6.63% in 2028/29. Council financial viability depends materially on property valuations and rental yields continuing at current levels.","category":"income","matters":"Council's financial sustainability is now contingent on continued commercial property performance; property market downturn or rental pressure would significantly worsen the underlying financing position","figures":"Financing costs reduced by 1.86pp (2026/27), 6.90pp (2027/28), 8.44pp (2028/29) when property income included","quote":"The investment properties have significant financing costs. However, these financing costs are significantly more than offset by the income they generate.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Enc. 1 for Treasury Management 2026/27 - Q1 April - June 2026","paper_url":"https://ashfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s38590/38590.pdf","headline":"General Fund financing costs rising from 11.31% of net revenue (2026/27) to 15.07% by 2028/29","detail":"Capital programme investment is driving increasing debt service costs as a proportion of core council revenue. The ratio jumps from 11.31% (2026/27) to 14.61% (2027/28) and then to 15.07% (2028/29), reflecting both reduced government funding and rising Minimum Revenue Provision charges. This leaves progressively less revenue available for delivering services.","category":"capital","matters":"Rising debt service burden indicates tightening financial position and reduces financial flexibility; at 15%, financing costs consume a material portion of controllable revenue","figures":"11.31% (2026/27), 14.61% (2027/28), 15.07% (2028/29)","quote":"The Ratio for the General Fund increases from 2027/28 as a result of reduced Government funding and increased MRP charges due to Capital Programme Developments.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Ashfield","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-25","since_iso":"2026-08-26","distress":false,"headline":"Minimum Revenue Provision policy for pre-2008 borrowing revised to 50-year annuity method, replacing 4% reducing balance approach which extended over 300 years","lead":"More prudent MRP approach accelerates retirement of historic debt and improves long-term financial sustainability through faster de-leveraging","also":["5 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit Committee on 2026-11-05","n_findings":6,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Enc. 1 for Treasury Management 2026/27 - Q1 April - June 2026","top_paper_url":"https://ashfield.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s38590/38590.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":78,"news_drafts_total":1}}